il  is suitable  peanuts are stacked like this for drying  during early fall  from virginia to texas  everyone pitches in to get the crop  harvested women folks drive tractors  the kids do what they can  and when threshing starts those goobers  really begin to pile up  of course to get this crop planted and  harvested takes real planning and  cooperation whole communities get  together to share new machinery  machines which just have to be shared as  there aren't enough to go around  steel that used to go into farm  machinery is now being made into ships  and tanks  ships and tanks and guns that we're  using to put the squeeze on hitler and  hirohito just like we're putting the  squeeze on mr goober to get that  valuable oil  after the oil is out what's left is made  into meal which is used to help feed the  extra livestock we're raising these days  nothing wasted  and that oil here it is  and it's one of the best cooking oils  known  and there's still some peanuts left for  those who like to munch them at the  movies  and of course plenty left to supply  peanut butter to the army because the  army considers it a vital food  especially for men in foreign fields  so you see this and every other farmer  with his field of peanuts is really a  defense industry  another of the farmer's important jobs  is that of easing the transportation  problem of helping trains  like this  carry more and more of the materials of  war  but let's hear from an expert on this  subject  no  well no  i'm not really an expert on  transportation  but i do feel that we women folks can do  a lot to help  by just growing and canning enough for  our own families for an entire year  that means things don't have to be  shipped to us that makes it easier for  the army to get the materials they need  to fight with  and besides that saves the tin cans for  the city folks who just naturally can't  grow and can things for themselves  and you know  i always feel there's more downright  goodness to the things i put up myself  that's the southern farmer's job in the  war  food and scrap oils and gunpowder bombs  and clothing  all war necessities depending on the  fields of cotton and peanuts the gardens  and pastures the tractors and mules and  depending on the men and women  work which may not seem as exciting as  building 30 ton tanks or four motor  bombers yet even in that the building of  four motored bombers our farmers have a  part  these young men your sons and the sons  of your neighbors in maine and minnesota  and oklahoma are about to take off on a  bombing rate over hitler's europe  about to take off in planes that every  farmer in america has helped to build  from your fields come the explosives in  these bombs the ammunition for these  guns the insulation for miles of wires  the plastics and the instruments and  gadgets facing the pilot oils and  fabrics and paints the very fields of  america and the heart of our hills  forged by the skill of our workmen into  weapons of freedom and if we from day to  day do our jobs as well as those who  fight the cause of freedom for all men  is well served
 

